in Daniel Defoe's famous novel Robinson  Crusoe we read of swashbuckling stories  of adventures on the high seas we read  of trials mishaps misadventures Robinson  Crusoe enact some curious habits upon  himself every night as he goes to bed he  sleeps on the beach he points his toes  towards the ocean every morning the tide  rolls in wets his feet he rises it wakes  him up now while the tide may not be  exactly regular in this way Robinson  Crusoe orders his life through habits he  sets rules for himself but let's not  just a listen to this story let us ask  why Robinson Crusoe is in the state of  nature isolated he has no social  responsibility and answers to no man but  himself but he understands the crucial  lesson that rules help us better govern  our lives and help us to live more  ordered and more fulfilling lives when  we come together in society especially  political society we enact certain rules  with how we interact with one another  how we treat one another and we set  limits on how those rules can be enacted  to begin with however in 21st century  America we have fundamentally changed  what the limits are on the rules that  are created for how we govern ourselves  and this leads us to a point where  ultimately we live under a different  contract than that under which we first  agreed to coexist as a society so let us  ask of ourselves as we asked of Robinson  Crusoe why to answer this question we  must examine three things first why do  we make these rules in the first place  secondly what are these rules and what  are their limits and finally how have  they changed if at all before we can  truly understand the impact that this  has on our everyday lives James Buchanan  a Nobel Prize winning economist argues  in his works reasons of rules and limits  of Liberty that mankind comes together  not as Locke says to defend our rights  in society but to enact an external  enforcement agency for the rules with  how we treat one another  now while these two philosophies may  have the same end we see that there are  two different types of rules under  buchanans line of reasoning there are  the rules by which we engage with one  another so I notice dr. Ahrens tie for  instance may not be quite as shiny or  quite as bright as mine but he can't  just take mine I get to protect it those  are the rules under which we live I  apologize dr. I'm but those are the  rules but there's another set of rules a  set of rules that govern how we can  create those rules to begin with and  this is the constitutional side of the  rules this set of rules governs the  government of the United States of  America in the form of the United States  Constitution Buchanan calls this the  paradox of governance every night with  good intentions I set an alarm clock  before I go to bed and every morning I  curse that decision as i hit snooze at  one time i know that that is a good rule  for my life but there's a temporal  disconnect between that knowledge and  the actual action that results from it  wherein I may disagree with that rule  that I set up for myself now let's not  take this to the extreme to say that  once we enter government there may be  things that happen that we disagree with  them we have no recourse however the  paradox of governance shows us that it  is for this reason that we make rules in  the first place because we know that  eventually we won't agree with them but  if this is why we have these  constitutional rules to begin with what  are they in American government stall  work of American government is our  balance of power the limitations that  are placed upon this balance of power it  is the role of the legislative to create  these rules under which we live it is  the role of the judicial to discern  disputes to resolve differences within  these rules the executive has a very  unique position because the executive is  allowed to act outside and above these  rules John Locke argues that there are  many things that which law cannot  provide for and these necessarily must  be left to the discernment of him who  wields executive power in his hands  without taking this to the extreme there  are limits that our founders placed on  what this discernment entails an  American government the executive has  specific enumerated powers they are  allowed to act with what political  theorists call energy they must be an  energetic executive they must be able to  act to enforce the laws which we have  established through a sense of urgency  and a sense of expediency they have this  discernment in a state of emergency when  we're in war or when there's a natural  disaster of some kind but it is that  state of emergency that has changed you  see through our initial balance of power  the presidency of the United States of  America the executive power does not  really limit the other powers very much  Barack Obama to date through his seven  years in offices only signed three  vetoes on every single bill that has  come across his desk yet before 1893  when Cleveland first left office there  was no single president whoever averaged  more than 28 executive orders per year  to date this has snowballed to a total  of thirteen thousand six hundred and  ninety-one total executive orders signed  by the President of the United States of  America because no president since that  time has ever signed fewer than 30 per  year Barack Obama January 21st 2015  signed an executive order under which he  declares the Arctic to be and I quote of  strategic cultural economic and  environmental importance to the United  States of America and quote with this  executive order what he does is he  establishes a committee to take care of  preserving the polar ice caps to take  care of exploring and protecting the  Arctic now if this is a state of  emergency today what have we lost we  have lost the guarded cautious  deliberate approach to the rules under  which we live that the legislature  initially sought out to attain instead  anything that is deemed necessary by the  executive is left to the discernment of  the executive  this leads to sweeping reforms in health  care education welfare reform  immigration the list goes on but because  this fundamental state of emergency has  been redefined we have changed the  limits of the executive power of the  United States of America because we have  allowed them to have discernment in  areas in which they previously had no  discernment now the sign of inductive  reasoning leads itself to very obvious  conclusions if Barack Obama can declare  a polar emergency to save penguins and  dr. Arnn can declare a fashion emergency  from I tie the reasoning extended  extends itself in American government  now that may be a frivolous example but  this really is a serious matter the  rules under which the rules are created  by which we treat one another have  fundamentally been altered because their  limits have been changed and the powers  have become imbalanced the executive  power is no longer something that act  urgently in a state of emergency because  they can declare to be a state of  emergency that which previously was  known was not considered to be expedient  circumstances exigent situations because  of this Buchanan argues that we live  under a different contract than that  under which we initially came together  as a political society now cynical  economists may talk a lot about  contracts in the contract of our  Constitution but at the end of the day  the Constitution is more than that  because it is the rules by which we  engage with one another and if we allow  the limits of those rules to change if  we allow one power to become so expanded  that they can define for themselves what  their level of discernment is in  creating those rules then we've lost the  purpose of those rules to begin with  Robinson Crusoe may have set up rules  for himself to order his own life as we  all do with personal habits  but when we come together we must be  very careful about how those rules are  created to begin with because they are  very susceptible to change and we must  not allow those limits to be expanded
 

